This study aims to investigate whether exposure to fast-paced environments during childhood is associated with an increased risk of developing Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) in pupils. The research will employ a mixed-methods approach, combining both quantitative and qualitative data collection and analysis methods. The study will recruit a sample of 500 pupils from urban and rural schools in a developing country, with half exposed to fast-paced environments (e.g., through access to digital devices, video games, or high-traffic areas) and the other half in a slow-paced environment (e.g., through limited access to technology and quiet spaces). Pupils will be assessed using standardized ADHD diagnostic tools (e.g., Conners 3, ADHD Self-Report Scale) at baseline and after 12 months. Additionally, parental reports, teacher feedback, and pupil self-reports will be collected to provide contextual information. The study will analyze the data using regression models to determine whether fast-paced environmental exposure is a significant predictor of ADHD development. The findings will contribute to a better understanding of the environmental factors influencing ADHD risk and inform evidence-based interventions for early prevention.
